-\subsection{Minimum-Actuator Count for the Vastus Medialis Requirement}\label{sec:min_bpa}
-
-The extensor requirement is set by vastus medialis, the quadriceps muscle with the smallest maximum isometric force in the reference set, and even that weakest-quad envelope demands roughly twice the peak torque of the flexor requirement. For every combination of actuator count and tackle line parts, with the actuator size re-solved for each configuration so that no configuration inherits a resting length sized for a different transmission, the optimizer searched routes against the vastus medialis envelope at the pressure ceiling, sweeping one to three BPAs in parallel over direct drives and up to three tackle line parts, under both moment-arm treatments and both with and without the stiffness corrections. No configuration in the grid meets the envelope at every graded angle. The best design is three BPAs in parallel through a two-part tackle, solved stiffness-aware and graded on the cable moment arm: it meets four of the five graded angles with margins of $+56.3$, $+40.1$, $+35.4$, and $+1.0$ \unit{\newton\meter}, failing only at full extension, where the route geometry collapses toward zero torque for every configuration in the grid, so the shortfall there is a property of the routing family rather than of the actuator count. The cable moment arm, which wraps the path from the given muscle insertion to the patellofibular ring point $0.75$ \unit{\milli\metre} off the bone surfaces, exceeds the OpenSim moment arm throughout the range and dominates the comparison under both stiffness treatments; the direct-drive configurations peak at two of five graded angles regardless of count. The outcome matches the redesign decision: carrying the full vastus medialis envelope through this routing family requires at least the three-actuator scale, and even then the extension end of the range needs a routing change rather than more actuators.
